 decision/lost-endpoint-loses-event · A lost endpoint loses the event, not the turn — When a hook cannot reach its endpoint, the hook event is lost and the turn goes on.
+decision/mcp-call-deadline · MCP call deadline of 55 s — Every MCP frame tool call (send, wait, last, answer) is bounded by a compiled 55 s call deadline, above the 50 s wait cap and below the claude client's tool-call bound; at expiry the call's work is dropped and the tool returns instance-unreachable with detail call-deadline; list is not under it. · why: So every MCP tool call completes below the client's tool-call timeout. · guard: mcp_call_deadline_sits_between_the_wrapper_bounds_and_the_client_read
 decision/mcp-frame-tools-strict-modes-check · MCP frame tools refuse a strict-modes failure — Every MCP frame tool refuses a home or target instance directory that fails the strict-modes check with the instance-unreachable result and detail strict-modes-failed, sending no frame. · why: The handlers test expects the refusal before any snapshot read or frame, so an untrusted home never steers a frame to a wrapper.
@@ -137,4 +138,4 @@
 decision/mcp-stdin-oversize-refused-whole · Oversize MCP stdin line refused whole — An MCP stdin line longer than the frame cap reaches rmcp as no byte and ends the session as at a closed stdin; the line is never truncated and never skipped. · why: A truncated line could dispatch a padded valid request, and a skipped one leaves the peer unanswered with no signal; follows the wrapper channel's close-on-oversize rule.
-decision/mcp-unreachable-result-for-every-cause · MCP frame tools report every unreachable cause one way — Every unreachable cause an MCP frame tool meets surfaces as the instance-unreachable result with the cause in detail and no line of its own; every cause but endpoint-lost is found before any frame is sent. · why: MCP drivers branch on the one instance-unreachable error and its closed detail; in the MCP process the result is the only record of the cause.
-decision/mcp-wait-cap · MCP wait capped at 50 s — MCP `wait` sends `timeout_ms` as the smaller of the given value and 50 000 ms, and 50 000 ms when none is given. · why: The cap sits below the Claude CLI's stdio tool-call default, so the tool call never times out on the client first.
+decision/mcp-unreachable-result-for-every-cause · MCP frame tools report every unreachable cause one way — Every unreachable cause an MCP frame tool meets surfaces as the instance-unreachable result with the cause in detail and no line of its own; every cause but endpoint-lost and call-deadline is found before any frame is sent. · why: MCP drivers branch on the one instance-unreachable error and its closed detail; in the MCP process the result is the only record of the cause.
+decision/mcp-wait-cap · MCP wait capped at 50 s — MCP `wait` sends `timeout_ms` as the smaller of the given value and 50 000 ms, and 50 000 ms when none is given. · why: The cap sits below the 55 s MCP call deadline, itself below the client's tool-call bound, so a wait returns before either cuts the call.
 decision/ndjson-logs-and-atomic-snapshots · ndjson append logs plus atomic JSON snapshots — State is kept in ndjson append logs and atomic JSON snapshots, with std `File::lock` on separate `.lock` files; the log stays the source of truth. · why: The trail stays human-readable and `tail`-able, with no C in the build and no daemon; SQLite and redb were rejected.
